  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the compositional and structural diversity of MXenes and the properties that follow from that diversity. It first shows the general formula, with layered transition-metal carbides or nitrides built from different early transition metals and either C or N, emphasizing that many metal choices are possible. The central part compares several MXene atomic arrangements: a “common” form, ordered and disordered mixed-metal versions, a high-entropy form with several metals, and structures that differ in how surface terminations or metal layers are arranged. In these schematics, metal atoms occupy stacked layers around carbon or nitrogen, while the outer surfaces carry various terminal groups.\n\nA side view highlights possible adsorption or termination sites on the MXene surface, such as top, bridge, hcp, and fcc positions. Together, these panels convey that MXenes are not a single material but a broad family with many possible layer chemistries and atomic orderings.\n\nThe bottom row links this structural versatility to key material features: adjustable surface chemistry, tunable electronic band gap, coexistence of covalent and metallic bonding, many possible terminal groups, controllable stacked films with nanometer-scale spacing, and mechanical flexibility. Overall, the figure presents MXenes as highly designable 2D materials.",
